Nix uses call-by-need lazy evaluation with critical edge cases:

```nix
# Infinite recursion DETECTED at evaluation time (not runtime)
let infinite = infinite + 1; in infinite
# → error: infinite recursion encountered

# Thunk memoization (single evaluation)
let
  expensive = builtins.trace "EVALUATING" (builtins.fetchurl {
    url = "https://example.com/large.tar.gz";
    hash = "sha256-...";
  });
in {
  a = expensive;
  b = expensive;  # Same thunk – NOT re-evaluated
}

# Strictness operators (force evaluation)
# builtins.seq: evaluate first arg, return second
builtins.seq (builtins.trace "forced" 1) 2  # → logs "forced", returns 2

# builtins.deepSeq: recursively force entire value
builtins.deepSeq { a = 1; b = 2; } "done"  # Forces both attrs before returning "done"

# Critical edge case: lists vs attrsets
let
  lazyList = [ (builtins.trace "list elem" 1) ];
  forced = builtins.seq lazyList null;
in forced  # → DOES NOT trigger trace! Lists are shallowly lazy

let
  lazyAttrset = { x = builtins.trace "attr" 1; };
  forced = builtins.seq lazyAttrset null;
in forced  # → DOES trigger trace! Attrsets force keys on seq
```

⚠️ Nix detects recursion via call stack depth (default 10,000 frames) – NOT cycle detection in DAG
⚠️ Thunk memoization is PER-EVALUATION – separate `nix eval` invocations re-evaluate
⚠️ `builtins.seq` forces ONLY top-level structure – use `deepSeq` for nested values
⚠️ Lists are shallowly lazy: `[ (expensive) ]` evaluates element ONLY when accessed via `builtins.elemAt`
⚠️ Attrsets force ALL keys on `seq` – design implication for conditional attrsets:
```nix
# DANGEROUS: both branches evaluated!
{ } // (if cond then { a = expensive1; } else { b = expensive2; })

# SAFE: use functions to defer evaluation
let branch = if cond then (_: { a = expensive1; }) else (_: { b = expensive2; });
in branch {}
```

NixOS module system merges options from multiple modules using priority-based conflict resolution:

```nix
# Priority hierarchy (highest to lowest)
1. mkForce      – ignores all lower priorities
2. mkOverride   – overrides mkDefault/mkOption
3. literal value – e.g., `enable = true`
4. mkDefault    – only used if no higher priority value exists
5. mkOption     – declaration only (no value)

# Example: conflicting declarations
# module-a.nix
{ config, lib, ... }: {
  services.nginx.enable = lib.mkDefault true;
}

# module-b.nix
{ config, lib, ... }: {
  services.nginx.enable = false;  # Literal value > mkDefault
}

# Result: services.nginx.enable = false

# mkForce example (emergency override)
# emergency.nix
{ config, lib, ... }: {
  networking.firewall.enable = lib.mkForce false;  # Disables firewall REGARDLESS of other modules
}

# mkMerge example (combine lists without duplication)
{ config, lib, ... }: {
  environment.variables.PATH = lib.mkMerge [
    [ "/custom/bin" ]
    config.environment.variables.PATH  # Preserve existing PATH
  ];
}

# Advanced: type-dependent merging
# For attrsOf types, values merged recursively
services.openssh.settings = {
  PasswordAuthentication = lib.mkDefault false;
  PermitRootLogin = "no";
};
# Another module can override single attr:
services.openssh.settings.PasswordAuthentication = true;
# Result: { PasswordAuthentication = true; PermitRootLogin = "no"; }

# For listOf types, values concatenated
boot.kernelModules = [ "kvm-intel" ];
# Another module:
boot.kernelModules = [ "vfio-pci" ];
# Result: [ "kvm-intel" "vfio-pci" ]
```

⚠️ mkForce should ONLY be used in emergency recovery – breaks module composition guarantees
⚠️ mkDefault values visible in `nixos-option` but NOT in final config if overridden
⚠️ Recursive merging ONLY works for attrsets – lists always concatenate (no de-duplication)
⚠️ Module import order DOES NOT matter – priority system ensures deterministic resolution
⚠️ Debug merged config: `nix-instantiate --eval -E '(import <nixpkgs/nixos> { configuration = import ./configuration.nix; }).config.services.nginx.enable'`

Flakes implement reproducible dependency management via lock files and input schemas:

**Input schema types**:
| Schema | Example | Resolution |
|--------|---------|------------|
| `github:NixOS/nixpkgs/nixos-25.11` | GitHub repo + ref | API → commit hash |
| `git+https://example.com/repo` | Git URL | `git ls-remote` → HEAD |
| `path:/absolute/path` | Local path | Direct filesystem access |
| `git+file:///relative/path` | Relative path (Nix 2.26+) | Relative to flake root |
| `indirect:my-flake` | Registry lookup | `~/.config/nix/registry.json` |

**Critical: `follows` semantics**
```nix
# flake.nix
{
  inputs = {
    nixpkgs.url = "github:NixOS/nixpkgs/nixos-25.11";
    mylib.url = "github:user/mylib";
    mylib.inputs.nixpkgs.follows = "nixpkgs";  # ← KEY: reuse parent's nixpkgs
  };
}
```
Why `follows` matters:
- Without `follows`, mylib gets its OWN nixpkgs instance → diamond dependency problem
- With `follows`, mylib uses EXACT SAME nixpkgs commit as parent → single source of truth
- Prevents version skew (e.g., mylib built against nixpkgs-unstable while app uses 25.11)

**Registry mechanics**:
```json
// ~/.config/nix/registry.json
{
  "flakes": [
    {
      "from": { "type": "indirect", "id": "nixpkgs" },
      "to": { "type": "github", "owner": "NixOS", "repo": "nixpkgs", "ref": "nixos-25.11" }
    }
  ]
}
```
- Registry provides default resolution for indirect inputs
- Overrides via `nix registry add github:NixOS/nixpkgs github:user/fork`
- Registry NOT committed to repo – user-specific configuration

**Lock file integrity**:
```json
// flake.lock
{
  "nodes": {
    "nixpkgs": {
      "locked": {
        "lastModified": 1732982400,
        "narHash": "sha256-XXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXX=",
        "owner": "NixOS",
        "repo": "nixpkgs",
        "rev": "a1b2c3d4e5f6...",
        "type": "github"
      },
      "original": { "owner": "NixOS", "ref": "nixos-25.11", "repo": "nixpkgs", "type": "github" }
    }
  },
  "root": "root",
  "version": 7
}
```
Critical fields:
- `narHash`: cryptographic hash of ENTIRE flake output → integrity verification
- `rev`: exact Git commit → reproducibility
- `lastModified`: Unix timestamp → cache invalidation

⚠️ Flakes remain EXPERIMENTAL (RFC 0065 not finalized) – API may change pre-3.0 [[86]]
⚠️ `follows` breaks if intermediate flake removes input – fragile composition
⚠️ Registry lookups happen DURING EVALUATION – can fail if network unavailable
⚠️ Nix 2.26 added `nix flake lock --update-input nixpkgs` for targeted updates
⚠️ Lock file tampering detection: `nix flake check` verifies narHash against actual content

Nix interpreter (C++) implements lazy functional evaluation with specialized memory model:

**Value representation** (union type):
```cpp
enum ValueType {
  tInt, tBool, tString, tPath, tNull,
  tAttrs, tList, tLambda, tPrimOp,
  tThunk, tApp, tBlackhole
};

struct Value {
  ValueType type;
  union {
    int integer;
    bool boolean;
    const char *string;
    struct {
      Bindings *attrs;
      Env *env;  // Lexical environment for attrset values
    } attrs;
    struct {
      Value *elts;
      size_t size;
    } list;
    struct {
      ExprLambda *lambda;
      Env *env;
    } lambda;
    struct {
      Expr *expr;
      Env *env;
    } thunk;  // Unevaluated expression + environment
  };
};
```

**Thunk evaluation workflow**:
1. Access value of type `tThunk`
2. Check for blackhole (detects infinite recursion):
   ```cpp
   if (v->type == tBlackhole) throw EvalError("infinite recursion");
   v->type = tBlackhole;  // Mark as evaluating
   ```
3. Evaluate `expr` in `env` → new value `v2`
4. Update thunk in-place (memoization):
   ```cpp
   *v = *v2;  // Replace thunk with result
   ```
5. Return `v`

**Memory management**:
- No GC during evaluation – values allocated in arenas
- Arena per evaluation context (cleared after eval completes)
- Store paths managed separately by daemon (reference counted)
- Thunk memoization prevents re-evaluation but increases memory usage

**Critical performance characteristics**:
- Attrset lookup: O(log n) via sorted vector (not hash map)
- List concatenation (`++`): O(n) – creates new list
- String interpolation: O(n) with quadratic worst-case for nested interpolations
- Lambda application: creates new environment frame (O(1) allocation)

**Blackhole detection deep dive**:
```nix
# Infinite recursion example
let infinite = infinite + 1; in infinite
# Evaluation steps:
1. Create thunk for 'infinite'
2. Access 'infinite' → mark as blackhole
3. Evaluate 'infinite + 1' → access 'infinite' again
4. Detect blackhole → throw EvalError
```

⚠️ Thunk memoization is PER-EVALUATION – separate `nix eval` invocations re-evaluate
⚠️ Attrset keys sorted lexicographically – affects merge order in modules
⚠️ No tail call optimization – deep recursion hits stack limit (~10,000 frames)
⚠️ Nix 2.26 added arena allocator improvements – 15% faster evaluation for large flakes
⚠️ Memory leak vector: circular references in attrsets (rare – requires unsafe builtins)

Nix has NO static type system – all type checking happens at runtime:

**Dynamic typing mechanics**:
```nix
# All values carry type tag at runtime
let
  x = 42;          # tInt
  y = "hello";     # tString
  z = { a = 1; };  # tAttrs
in {
  # Type errors detected ONLY when operation attempted
  bad = x + y;  # ← Runtime error: cannot coerce string to int
}
```

**Type coercion rules** (implicit conversions):
| Operation | Allowed coercions | Example |
|-----------|-------------------|---------|
| `+` (string concat) | Any → string | `"value: " + 42` → `"value: 42"` |
| `+` (integer add) | int + int ONLY | `42 + 1` → `43` |
| Path interpolation | path → string | `"${./.}/file"` → `"/home/user/file"` |
| Boolean in condition | ANY → bool | `if 0 then ...` → false (0=false, non-0=true) |

**Critical coercion footguns**:
```nix
# Footgun 1: numbers vs strings in attrset keys
{ "42" = "string key"; }."42"  # Works
{ 42 = "int key"; }.42        # FAILS – keys always strings!

# Footgun 2: boolean coercion of empty strings
if "" then true else false  # → false (empty string = false)
if "0" then true else false # → true (non-empty string = true)

# Footgun 3: path vs string concatenation
"/nix/store/" + hash         # String concatenation (loses path properties)
"${pkgs.bash}/bin"          # Path interpolation (preserves references)
```

**Module system type checking** (ONLY place with static-ish types):
```nix
# options.nix
{ config, lib, ... }: {
  options.services.my-service = {
    enable = lib.mkEnableOption "my service";
    port = lib.mkOption {
      type = lib.types.port;  # ← Runtime type check on assignment
      default = 8080;
    };
    configFile = lib.mkOption {
      type = lib.types.path;  # ← Must be store path or source path
      description = "Config file path";
    };
  };
}

# Attempting invalid assignment:
services.my-service.port = "not a port";
# → Evaluation error: value is not a port
```

Type system limitations:
- Types checked ONLY at option assignment time
- No function signature types
- No generic types / parametric polymorphism
- Type errors reported with poor location info (often at merge time, not assignment)

⚠️ Nix language fundamentally untyped – all safety comes from runtime checks
⚠️ Module types are best-effort – can be bypassed via `lib.mkForce`
⚠️ Type errors often reported far from actual cause (due to lazy evaluation)
⚠️ Nix 2.26 added experimental `nix typecheck` command (very limited coverage)
⚠️ Community efforts: Nickel language (typed Nix alternative) – not production-ready

Overlays compose via function chaining with critical ordering semantics:

**Overlay composition mechanics**:
```nix
# overlays/default.nix
[
  (import ./overlay-a.nix)
  (import ./overlay-b.nix)
  (import ./overlay-c.nix)
]

# overlay-a.nix
final: prev: {
  hello = prev.hello.overrideAttrs (o: { version = "2.13"; });
}

# overlay-b.nix
final: prev: {
  # 'prev' includes overlay-a's modifications!
  hello = prev.hello.overrideAttrs (o: { meta.description = "Patched hello"; });
}

# overlay-c.nix
final: prev: {
  # 'prev' includes BOTH a and b
  my-package = final.callPackage ./my-package { };
}
```

**Critical ordering properties**:
1. Overlays applied LEFT TO RIGHT
2. Each overlay receives `prev` = result of previous overlays
3. `final` = fully composed package set (including ALL overlays)
4. Self-referential overlays possible but DANGEROUS:
```nix
# DANGEROUS: infinite recursion risk
selfRef: prev: {
  python3 = prev.python3.override {
    packageOverrides = selfRef: prev: {
      # 'selfRef' refers to THIS overlay → recursion!
      requests = prev.requests.overridePythonAttrs (_: { version = "2.32.0"; });
    };
  };
}
```

**Safe self-reference pattern**:
```nix
# SAFE: use final for self-reference
final: prev: {
  python3 = prev.python3.override {
    packageOverrides = final: prev: {
      # 'final' = fully composed set – no recursion
      requests = prev.requests.overridePythonAttrs (_: { version = "2.32.0"; });
    };
  };
}
```

**Performance implications**:
- Each overlay creates NEW attrset copy → O(n) per overlay
- 10 overlays on 80k-package set → 800k attr copies → 2-3x eval slowdown
- Mitigation: combine related overlays into single file
- Critical path: `callPackage` in overlays (triggers eval of entire dependency tree)

**Advanced: conditional overlays**
```nix
# Only apply overlay if condition met
final: prev:
  if prev.stdenv.hostPlatform.isLinux
  then { linux-only = prev.callPackage ./linux-only { }; }
  else {}
```

⚠️ Overlay order matters – later overlays see results of earlier ones
⚠️ NEVER modify `prev` directly – always return new attrset (`final: prev: { ... }`)
⚠️ Self-referential overlays cause infinite recursion if not using `final`
⚠️ Overlays evaluated LAZILY – performance hit only when accessed
⚠️ Nixpkgs 25.11 includes 150+ community overlays – composition can get complex [[11]]

Derivation composition has subtle limitations that trip up senior developers:

**overrideAttrs limitations**:
```nix
# Original derivation
stdenv.mkDerivation {
  pname = "my-app";
  version = "1.0";
  src = ./src;
  buildInputs = [ pkgs.openssl ];
  configureFlags = [ "--with-openssl" ];
}

# overrideAttrs ONLY overrides top-level attrs
overrideAttrs (old: {
  version = "1.1";
  # buildInputs NOT overridden – must explicitly reassign
  buildInputs = old.buildInputs ++ [ pkgs.zlib ];
  # configureFlags completely replaced – not merged!
  configureFlags = old.configureFlags ++ [ "--with-zlib" ];
})
```

Critical limitations:
1. **No deep merging**: Nested attrsets completely replaced
   ```nix
   meta = { license = licenses.mit; };
   overrideAttrs (_: { meta = { homepage = "https://example.com"; }; })
   # Result: { homepage = ... } – LICENSE LOST!
   ```

2. **Phase functions not composable**: `buildPhase` completely replaced
   ```nix
   buildPhase = "make";
   overrideAttrs (_: { buildPhase = "make test"; })
   # Original "make" LOST – no way to extend
   ```

3. **Dependencies not automatically propagated**: Adding lib to `buildInputs` doesn't add its deps to output

**callPackage mechanics**:
```nix
# default.nix
{ stdenv, fetchurl, openssl ? null, ... }@args:
stdenv.mkDerivation {
  # ...
  buildInputs = [ ] ++ lib.optional (openssl != null) openssl;
}

# Usage
pkgs.callPackage ./default.nix { openssl = pkgs.openssl_3; }
# How it works:
1. Inspect function signature → extract arg names
2. Match args against pkgs attrset:
   - stdenv → pkgs.stdenv
   - fetchurl → pkgs.fetchurl
   - openssl → overridden value (pkgs.openssl_3)
3. Call function with merged args
```

**Recursive derivation pattern** (advanced):
```nix
# Derivation that depends on its own output
selfRef = stdenv.mkDerivation {
  pname = "self-ref";
  version = "1.0";
  # Use placeholder during eval
  src = if selfRef == null then ./dummy else selfRef;
  # Actual build uses real selfRef
  buildPhase = ''
    cp ${selfRef}/bin/tool ./tool-v2
  '';
}
# Requires --allow-unsafe-native-code-during-evaluation (DANGEROUS)
```

⚠️ overrideAttrs cannot extend phase functions – must copy entire phase
⚠️ callPackage signature inspection fails for `@`-patterns with `...`
⚠️ Recursive derivations break reproducibility – avoid except for bootstrapping
⚠️ Nix 2.26 added `overrideDerivation` (deprecated) → use `overrideAttrs` exclusively
⚠️ Composition performance: each override creates new derivation object – avoid deep chains

Nix `let` expressions implement lexical scoping with subtle shadowing semantics:

**Lexical scope rules**:
```nix
let
  a = 1;
  b = let
    a = 2;  # Shadows outer 'a'
  in a + 1;  # → 3 (uses inner a)
in a + b  # → 1 + 3 = 4 (outer a + b result)
```

**Shadowing behavior**:
- Inner bindings shadow outer bindings with same name
- Shadowing is LEXICAL – based on source structure, not evaluation order
- No dynamic scope – functions capture environment at definition time

**Recursion in let bindings**:
```nix
# Direct recursion (allowed)
let
  factorial = n: if n <= 1 then 1 else n * factorial (n - 1);
in factorial 5  # → 120

# Mutual recursion (allowed)
let
  even = n: if n == 0 then true else odd (n - 1);
  odd = n: if n == 0 then false else even (n - 1);
in even 4  # → true

# Self-reference in attrsets (requires 'rec')
rec {
  a = 1;
  b = a + 1;  # 'a' visible due to 'rec'
}
```

**Critical edge cases**:

1. **Forward references**:
```nix
# ILLEGAL: forward reference
let
  a = b + 1;  # Error: undefined variable 'b'
  b = 2;
in a

# LEGAL: mutual recursion via functions
let
  a = x: b x + 1;
  b = x: x * 2;
in a 3  # → 7
```

2. **Attrset vs let scoping**:
```nix
# Attrset bindings NOT visible to each other
{ a = 1; b = a + 1; }  # Error: undefined variable 'a'

# 'rec' enables self-reference
rec { a = 1; b = a + 1; }  # → { a = 1; b = 2; }
```

3. **Function capture**:
```nix
let
  makeAdder = x: y: x + y;
  add5 = makeAdder 5;
in add5 3  # → 8 (captures x=5 lexically)
```

**Advanced patterns**:

✅ Nested let for complex logic:
```nix
let
  result =
    let
      temp1 = expensiveComputation1;
      temp2 = expensiveComputation2 temp1;
    in finalComputation temp2;
in result
```

✅ Let bindings for configuration:
```nix
let
  cfg = {
    port = 8080;
    host = "localhost";
  };
in {
  services.nginx.virtualHosts.${cfg.host} = {
    listen = [ { port = cfg.port; } ];
  };
}
```

⚠️ Let bindings evaluated LAZILY – only when accessed
⚠️ No block scope – entire let body shares same scope
⚠️ Shadowing can cause subtle bugs – prefer distinct names
⚠️ Recursion limited by stack depth (~10,000 frames)
⚠️ Nix 2.26 added tail call optimization for simple recursions (experimental)

NixOS module system includes sophisticated type system for option validation:

**Built-in type constructors**:
```nix
# Simple types
lib.types.bool
lib.types.int
lib.types.str
lib.types.path
lib.types.package

# Compound types
lib.types.listOf lib.types.str          # [ "a" "b" ]
lib.types.attrsOf lib.types.int         # { a = 1; b = 2; }
lib.types.nullOr lib.types.str          # null or string
lib.types.enum [ "a" "b" "c" ]          # One of literal values
lib.types.oneOf [ lib.types.int lib.types.str ]  # Union type

# Specialized types
lib.types.lines                         # Multi-line string (preserves newlines)
lib.types.raw                           # Bypasses type checking (DANGEROUS)
lib.types.unspecified                   # No type checking
```

**Type checking internals**:
```nix
# Type definition structure
{
  check = value: path: /* returns bool */;
  merge = loc: defs: /* merges multiple definitions */;
  getSubOptions = prefix: /* for attrsOf sub-options */;
  substSubModules = ...;  # For submodule types
}

# Example: listOf type implementation
listOf = elemType: {
  check = value: path:
    builtins.isList value &&
    builtins.all (elem: elemType.check elem (path ++ ["list element"])) value;
  merge = loc: defs:
    lib.concatMap (def: def.value) defs;  # Simple concatenation
};
```

**Custom type definition**:
```nix
# Define port number type (1-65535)
portType = with lib.types; let
  check = value: path:
    if builtins.isInt value && value >= 1 && value <= 65535
    then true
    else throw "Value at ${toString path} is not a valid port: ${toString value}";
in {
  inherit check;
  merge = loc: defs: lib.last defs;  # Last definition wins
  description = "TCP/UDP port number (1-65535)";
};

# Usage in module
options.my-service.port = {
  type = portType;
  default = 8080;
};
```

**Critical edge cases**:

1. **attrsOf with submodule**:
```nix
# Virtual hosts with per-host config
options.services.nginx.virtualHosts = {
  type = lib.types.attrsOf (lib.types.submodule ({ config, ... }: {
    options.enable = lib.types.bool;
    options.root = lib.types.path;
  }));
};

# Usage:
services.nginx.virtualHosts."example.com" = {
  enable = true;
  root = "/var/www/example";
};
```

2. **Type coercion**:
```nix
# Some types coerce values automatically
lib.types.int.check "42" []  # → true (string "42" coerced to int)
```

3. **Merge strategies per type**:
- `listOf`: concatenates lists
- `attrsOf`: merges attrsets recursively
- Simple types: last definition wins

⚠️ Type errors reported at merge time – often far from actual assignment
⚠️ `lib.types.raw` bypasses ALL checking – security risk if used carelessly
⚠️ Custom types cannot access module config – only value being checked
⚠️ Nix 2.26 added `lib.types.functionTo` for function signature types (experimental)
⚠️ Type checking happens during EVALUATION – not at build time

Nixpkgs `meta` attribute set provides critical package metadata:

**License representation**:
```nix
meta = with lib.licenses; {
  # Single license
  license = gpl3;

  # Multiple licenses (user can choose)
  license = [ gpl3 mit ];

  # AND combination (must comply with all)
  license = lib.licenses.licenseGroup [
    gpl3
    lgpl21Plus
  ];

  # Custom license
  license = lib.licenses.free "Custom permissive license";
};
```

License structure:
```nix
{
  shortName = "GPL-3.0";
  spdxId = "GPL-3.0-only";
  url = "https://www.gnu.org/licenses/gpl-3.0.html";
  free = true;  # FSF/OSI approved
  redistributable = true;  # Can redistribute binaries
}
```

**Maintainer representation**:
```nix
meta.maintainers = with lib.maintainers; [
  eelco      # Nix creator
  grahamc    # NixOS Foundation president
  yourname   # Add yourself!
];

# Maintainer definition (pkgs/top-level/maintainers.nix)
{
  yourname = {
    email = "you@example.com";
    github = "yourgithub";
    keys = [ "ssh-ed25519 AAAA..." ];
  };
}
```

**Platform support**:
```nix
meta.platforms = lib.platforms.linux ++ lib.platforms.darwin;
# Or explicit list:
meta.platforms = [ "x86_64-linux" "aarch64-linux" "x86_64-darwin" ];

# Broken on specific platforms
meta.broken = stdenv.isDarwin;  # Mark broken on macOS
meta.broken = stdenv.hostPlatform.isMusl;  # Broken on musl
```

**Critical meta fields**:
| Field | Purpose | Required? |
|-------|---------|-----------|
| `description` | One-line package description | Yes |
| `homepage` | Project URL | Yes |
| `license` | SPDX-compatible license | Yes |
| `maintainers` | Package maintainers | No (but encouraged) |
| `platforms` | Supported platforms | No (inferred if missing) |
| `broken` | Mark package broken | No |
| `mainProgram` | Default binary for `nix run` | No |

**Querying meta attributes**:
```bash
# From CLI
nix eval nixpkgs#hello.meta.description
# → "A program that produces a familiar, friendly greeting"

nix search nixpkgs hello
# Shows description + license
```

⚠️ Missing `license` field blocks Hydra builds (legal requirement)
⚠️ `broken = true` skips package in `nix-env -qa` by default (use `--all` to see)
⚠️ Platform support inferred from stdenv – but explicit declaration preferred
⚠️ Nix 2.26 added SPDX 3.20 support – all licenses mapped to SPDX IDs
⚠️ Meta attributes evaluated during PACKAGE DEFINITION – not per-derivation

Nixpkgs package composition uses function application with override mechanics:

**callPackage mechanics**:
```nix
# default.nix
{ stdenv, fetchurl, openssl ? null, ... }@args:
stdenv.mkDerivation { /* ... */ }

# How callPackage works:
1. Inspect function signature → extract arg names
2. Match args against pkgs attrset:
   - stdenv → pkgs.stdenv
   - fetchurl → pkgs.fetchurl
   - openssl → pkgs.openssl (default) OR overridden value
3. Call function with merged args

# Usage
pkgs.callPackage ./default.nix { openssl = pkgs.openssl_3; }
```

**Override patterns**:

✅ Simple override:
```nix
pkgs.hello.override { stdenv = pkgs.clangStdenv; }
```

✅ overrideAttrs (preferred):
```nix
pkgs.hello.overrideAttrs (old: {
  version = "2.13";
  src = pkgs.fetchurl { /* new src */ };
  buildInputs = old.buildInputs ++ [ pkgs.zlib ];
})
```

✅ Deep override (nested attrsets):
```nix
pkgs.python3.override {
  packageOverrides = self: super: {
    requests = super.requests.overridePythonAttrs (old: {
      version = "2.32.0";
      src = pkgs.fetchPypi { /* new src */ };
    });
  };
}
```

**Composition chains**:
```nix
# Chain multiple overrides
pkgs.hello
  .overrideAttrs (_: { version = "2.13"; })
  .overrideAttrs (old: { meta.description = "Patched hello"; })
  .override { stdenv = pkgs.clangStdenv; }

# Result: all overrides applied sequentially
```

**Critical limitations**:

1. **No deep merging**:
```nix
# overrideAttrs replaces entire attrset
meta = { license = licenses.mit; };
overrideAttrs (_: { meta = { homepage = "https://example.com"; }; })
# Result: { homepage = ... } – LICENSE LOST!

# Must manually merge:
overrideAttrs (old: {
  meta = old.meta // { homepage = "https://example.com"; };
})
```

2. **Phase functions not composable**:
```nix
buildPhase = "make";
overrideAttrs (_: { buildPhase = "make test"; })
# Original "make" LOST – no way to extend
```

3. **Dependencies not automatically propagated**:
```nix
# Adding lib to buildInputs doesn't add its deps to output
overrideAttrs (old: {
  buildInputs = old.buildInputs ++ [ pkgs.zlib ];
})
# Must also add to propagatedBuildInputs if needed at runtime
```

⚠️ overrideAttrs cannot extend phase functions – must copy entire phase
⚠️ callPackage signature inspection fails for complex patterns (e.g., `@` with `...`)
⚠️ Deep override chains create new derivation objects – avoid excessive chaining
⚠️ Nix 2.26 added `composeDerivation` for safer composition (experimental)
⚠️ Composition performance: each override creates new derivation – O(n) overhead

Nix has subtle coercion semantics that create footguns even for seniors:

**String context (hidden dependencies)**:
```nix
# Path interpolation adds "context" – hidden store path reference
let
  bash = /nix/store/abc...-bash-5.2;
  s = "${bash}/bin/bash";
in s
# 's' is string BUT carries context: [ "/nix/store/abc...-bash-5.2" ]

# Context preserved through string operations
let s2 = s + " --version"; in s2
# Context still present!

# Context LOST on explicit coercion
let s3 = builtins.toString s; in s3
# Context stripped – now plain string
```

**Critical footgun: context leakage**:
```nix
# DANGEROUS: context leaks into derivation name
let
  secret = /nix/store/secret-key;
  name = "my-app-${secret}";  # Context leaks!
in stdenv.mkDerivation {
  pname = name;  # → store path includes secret!
  # Result: /nix/store/...-my-app-/nix/store/secret-key
}

# SAFE: strip context explicitly
let
  secret = /nix/store/secret-key;
  name = "my-app-${builtins.baseNameOf (builtins.toString secret)}";
in stdenv.mkDerivation { pname = name; }
```

**__toString anti-pattern**:
```nix
# Custom toString via __toString attr
let
  myType = {
    a = 1;
    __toString = self: "MyType(${toString self.a})";
  };
in "${myType}"  # → "MyType(1)"

# DANGEROUS: __toString can execute arbitrary code
let
  dangerous = {
    __toString = self: builtins.trace "EXFILTRATING" "value";
  };
in "${dangerous}"  # → logs trace during string coercion!
```

**Coercion footguns**:

1. **Boolean coercion surprises**:
```nix
if 0 then true else false  # → false (0 = false)
if 1 then true else false  # → true (non-zero = true)
if "" then true else false # → false (empty string = false)
if "0" then true else false # → true (non-empty string = true)
```

2. **Path vs string concatenation**:
```nix
# Breaks reference tracking
bad = "/nix/store/" + hash + "-pkg";

# Preserves references
good = "/nix/store/${hash}-pkg";
```

3. **Number coercion in attr keys**:
```nix
{ 42 = "value"; }.42    # Works – number coerced to string
{ "42" = "value"; }.42  # Also works – same key
```

⚠️ String context causes hidden dependencies – breaks GC/refcounting
⚠️ __toString enables code execution during string coercion – security risk
⚠️ Context leakage into derivation names leaks secrets into store paths
⚠️ Nix 2.26 added `builtins.stripContext` to explicitly remove context
⚠️ Best practice: avoid string interpolation for non-path values – use `toString` explicitly

Senior maintainers avoid these anti-patterns that break nixpkgs:

**Anti-pattern 1: `broken = true` as TODO marker**
```nix
# DANGEROUS: marks package broken globally
meta.broken = true;  # Package disappears from nix search!

# SAFE: use platform-specific broken
meta.broken = stdenv.isDarwin;  # Only broken on macOS

# SAFE: use meta.knownBugs for non-breaking issues
meta.knownBugs = "Fails test suite on ARM";
```
Why dangerous:
- `broken = true` hides package from ALL users
- Breaks reverse dependencies silently
- Should ONLY be used for truly broken packages

**Anti-pattern 2: Mass rebuilds without staging**
```nix
# DANGEROUS: glibc update merged directly to master
stdenv.glibc = glibc_2_40;
# → 15,000 rebuilds – breaks master for days

# SAFE: merge to staging first
# PR against staging branch → weekly merge window
```
Why dangerous:
- Breaks master for all contributors
- Overwhelms Hydra build farm
- Causes merge conflicts for other PRs

**Anti-pattern 3: License violations**
```nix
# DANGEROUS: missing license field
meta = {
  description = "My package";
  homepage = "https://example.com";
  # NO license field!
};

# SAFE: always specify license
meta = with lib.licenses; {
  description = "My package";
  homepage = "https://example.com";
  license = mit;
};
```
Why dangerous:
- Hydra refuses to build packages without license
- Legal liability for redistributing unlicensed code
- Blocks binary cache population

**Anti-pattern 4: Impure fetch during eval**
```nix
# DANGEROUS: network access during evaluation
src = builtins.fetchurl {
  url = "https://github.com/user/repo/archive/master.tar.gz";
  sha256 = "...";
};
# Fetches LATEST master – not reproducible!

# SAFE: pin exact commit + hash
src = fetchFromGitHub {
  owner = "user";
  repo = "repo";
  rev = "a1b2c3d4";
  hash = "sha256-...";
};
```

**Anti-pattern 5: Overly broad maintainership**
```nix
# DANGEROUS: claiming maintainership for entire ecosystem
meta.maintainers = [ maintainers.you ];  # For every Python package

# SAFE: maintain only packages you actively use/test
meta.maintainers = [ ];  # No maintainer better than fake maintainer
```

⚠️ `broken = true` should be last resort – prefer platform-specific broken or knownBugs
⚠️ Mass rebuilds (>200 packages) MUST go through staging branch
⚠️ License field mandatory for Hydra builds – no exceptions
⚠️ Impure fetches break reproducibility – always pin exact versions
⚠️ Nixpkgs maintainership is VOLUNTARY – don't claim packages you won't maintain
